// Distributed schedule: 4 compute workers, async input streaming, sync output.
//
// Same algorithm as naive.sched.nuc, different mapping. The differential
// test in CI must show this produces bit-identical output to the naive
// schedule for the same input.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    place load_image on host;
    place save_image on host;
    place blur3      on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Outer y-loop: the original `loop y : partition=rows;` directive
    // is HELD BACK (commented below) — TASK-0258 landed the consumer
    // (passes/partition_rows.rs, this cycle) but the algo's y-loop
    // walks 1..H-1 = 1..15 (length 14), which is NOT divisible by 4
    // workers (14 / 4 = 3 rem 2). partition_rows's first-cut policy
    // rejects non-divisible ranges with PartitionRowsError::NonDivisible
    // (same precedent as partition_workers — TASK-0212). Restoring the
    // directive verbatim today would make every `nucleus build` of
    // this schedule fail at compile time, which is the right behaviour
    // for fail-fast but the wrong default for an example schedule that
    // is meant to demonstrate the distributed shape.
    //
    // History:
    //   - Pre-cycle-70: `loop y : partition=rows;` was inert — parsed
    //     and lowered, but no downstream pass consumed it (silent
    //     no-op).
    //   - TASK-0249 (cycle 70) closed the silent-drop by making
    //     partition=rows reject at sched-lower as
    //     SchedLowerErrorKind::UnsupportedPartitionKind. The directive
    //     was removed from this file to keep the schedule lowering.
    //   - TASK-0250 (cycle 76, decision option b) deferred the
    //     partition-policy choice until either a consumer landed or
    //     the cell became [[required]].
    //   - TASK-0258 (this cycle) lands the consumer; partition=rows
    //     now lowers cleanly at sched-lower and the partition_rows
    //     pass row-bands the outer axis of a 2D nest across the place
    //     set's workers. But the H=16 / y-loop=1..15 = length-14
    //     divisibility constraint blocks restoration of the directive
    //     as-is.
    //
    // Unblockers (any of):
    //   (a) Remainder policy for partition_rows (shared follow-up with
    //       partition_workers — both passes hit the same first-cut
    //       limit). Filed as TASK-0258 follow-up (last-worker-gets-
    //       remainder, or trailing-partial sibling to block_transform).
    //   (b) Halo inference (TASK-0260): when halo widths land, the
    //       schedule needs to be reworked anyway to carry halo
    //       transfers, and the divisibility choice can be revisited.
    //   (c) Shrinking the place set to a divisor of 14 (e.g. {w0, w1};
    //       14 / 2 = 7 exact) — would lose the four-way distributed
    //       shape this example is meant to demonstrate.
    //
    // Once one of those unblockers lands, uncomment the directive
    // below:
    //
    //     loop y : partition=rows;
    //
    // Cell remains [[skip]] across all 4 tier-1 backends regardless,
    // for the sibling gaps the e2e-matrix.toml entries cite (TASK-0117
    // + halo on pthreads-sync / mp-tcp-bufsync; TASK-0181 capability
    // mismatch on pthreads-async; TASK-0042.05 multi-worker codegen on
    // mp-tcp-event). When any of those gaps closes and the cell
    // becomes [[required]], TASK-0260 (halo inference) is the
    // dominant blocker before bit-identical comparison is possible —
    // pure partition=rows without halo synthesis produces wrong output
    // at row-band boundaries.

    // Inner loop: SIMD-friendly, with the reuse pattern that was the
    // unshipped piece in the 2013 thesis (slide a 3-wide window in x,
    // recycle 2 of 3 reads each step).
    loop x : block=64, vectorize=8, reuse;

    // host -> workers: pipelined async streaming, two row-bands in flight.
    // Event-driven notification (the backend must support it; a sync-only
    // backend rejects this schedule at compile time, by design).
    transfer img_in  : async, buffer=2, notify=event;

    // workers -> host: simple blocking write-back. Host waits for all
    // workers to finish before save_image runs.
    transfer img_out : sync;
}
